<section class="soc-sec"><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Sources and further reading</h2><ul class="essay-refs"><li>Edgerton, R. B. (2010) The Fall of the Asante Empire: The Hundred-Year War for Africa's Gold Coast. New York: Simon &amp; Schuster.</li><li>McCaskie, T. C. (2003) State and Society in Pre-colonial Asante. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.</li><li>Perbi, A. A. (2004) A History of Indigenous Slavery in Ghana: From the 15th to the 19th Century. Accra: Sub-Saharan Publishers.</li><li>Wilks, I. (1989) Asante in the Nineteenth Century: The Structure and Evolution of a Political Order. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.</li><li>Wilks, I. (1993) Forests of Gold: Essays on the Akan and the Kingdom of Asante. Athens: Ohio University Press.</li></ul><p class="soc-method">The shares are of the whole population, children included. Where a scholar has counted or estimated a rank, its share follows that work, named on the rank; where no count survives, the share is an estimate, marked est. and drawn dashed, made from what the rank was and did, from related figures and from the same rank in comparable societies, and its note says what it rests on. Population: approximately, for the kingdom at its height, as general accounts give it, without a named estimate behind the figure.</p></section>
