ission.","kid":"Police with long hair guarded Kumasi, and road wardens checked travellers and collected tolls.","rise":"These forces served the Asantehene and his government directly; the sources say little about how their men were chosen.","why":"The metropolitan districts had several police forces.","people":[]},{"n":8,"lv":5,"band":"commoners","name":"Free Asante","loc":"Amanfo","term":"odikro (village chief), abusua panyin (head of a lineage)","lbl":"About 84% (the rest, est.)","w":84.3,"firm":1,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/ashanti-8.jpg","you":"You farm yams and plantains with your mother’s lineage under your village chief, wear cotton, and since Osei Kwame Panyin’s law no one may sell you into slavery.","kid":"Most Asante were farmers who grew yams and plantains and lived in villages led by a chief.","rise":"Trade that served the state could make a commoner an obirempon, and since Osei Kwadwo’s reign the king’s offices went by ability; a capital crime could bring death, though banishment or imprisonment was the more common penalty.","why":"Most people were free Asante, the amanfo, citizens of the metropolitan states, and most of them farmed.","people":[]},{"n":9,"lv":6,"band":"enslaved","name":"Enslaved people","term":"-","lbl":"c. 10–20% (est.)","w":15,"firm":1,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/ashanti-9.jpg","you":"You were taken captive in war and now work on an Asante master’s farm; you may acquire wealth of your own and ask for another master if he mistreats you, but at his funeral you could be killed to follow him.","kid":"Enslaved people were owned by others and made to work without pay; most were captured in wars.","rise":"Capture in war and tribute made people slaves; a master who married an enslaved woman could pass part of his property and status to their children. When Britain conquered Asante it let the chiefs keep their slaves, and the ban of 1908 was enforced only from the 1920s.","why":"During the Atlantic slave trade Asante wa